WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.828
A logistics company's contract team asked the model: list the main data from this

00:00:06.828 --> 00:00:07.151
file.

00:00:07.451 --> 00:00:11.060
And the answer came back as a friendly little paragraph.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.558
The contract is with a carrier called Aurora.

00:00:14.858 --> 00:00:17.799
Total amount, twelve thousand four hundred dollars.

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:22.017
Starts in January, automatic renewal, twelve months.

00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:25.163
A regular expression pulled out the total.

00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:27.049
And it worked for weeks.

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:31.061
Then one day the model wrote the amount in words.

00:00:31.361 --> 00:00:34.323
Twelve thousand four hundred dollars, spelled out.

00:00:34.973 --> 00:00:37.501
And for the start date, just January.

00:00:38.151 --> 00:00:42.383
The system stored the total as empty, and the start date as empty.

00:00:42.683 --> 00:00:46.545
Nobody noticed until finance reconciled the next month.

00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:50.488
And the parser also let through things that only looked right.

00:00:50.788 --> 00:00:55.677
A contract ending in one year and starting in the next went in, because the text had

00:00:55.677 --> 00:00:58.703
both dates, and the regular expression grabbed both.

00:00:59.003 --> 00:01:04.475
In this lesson, you'll do the refactoring that ends this, safely, with a measurement

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:05.583
before and after.

00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:08.807
The solution has three moves.

00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:12.236
Define a schema for what this step outputs.

00:01:12.886 --> 00:01:17.940
Ask the model to answer in that schema, using the API's structured output feature.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.192
Not by asking in the prompt.

00:01:20.842 --> 00:01:24.305
And validate the result in code, before it moves on.

00:01:25.505 --> 00:01:29.033
Before, the format is a convention nobody wrote down.

00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:34.712
The model writes text, a regular expression guesses, and whatever it finds goes into

00:01:34.712 --> 00:01:35.417
the system.

00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:36.961
Including nothing.

00:01:37.611 --> 00:01:41.487
After, the format is a contract, checked at both ends.

00:01:41.787 --> 00:01:45.419
Same extraction call, now with a schema attached.

00:01:46.069 --> 00:01:49.300
The API returns an object that follows the schema.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.129
Right types, required fields present.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:57.866
Code checks the rules a schema can't express, like dates that make sense together.

00:01:58.516 --> 00:02:01.529
And only valid data reaches the destination.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:04.915
Failures go to review, with the reason attached.

00:02:05.565 --> 00:02:08.394
Here's the same contract, now as an object.

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:12.553
The tax ID, digits only, because the schema asked for that.

00:02:12.853 --> 00:02:15.180
The total as an integer, in cents.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.546
Dates in a fixed format.

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:20.305
Automatic renewal, true or false.

00:02:20.955 --> 00:02:22.636
No twelve thousand in words.

00:02:22.936 --> 00:02:28.268
And if the file has no end date, that field comes back empty, explicitly.

00:02:28.568 --> 00:02:33.085
Not as a phrase like open ended term that the old parser never expected.

00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:37.939
With free text, the format is an implicit promise.

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:41.903
The model usually writes it one way, and your code counts on that.

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:46.054
With structured output, the format becomes part of the call.

00:02:46.354 --> 00:02:51.695
The API constrains what the model can generate, token by token, so the answer always

00:02:51.695 --> 00:02:54.746
closes into valid data with the required fields.

00:02:55.396 --> 00:02:58.013
The schema also improves extraction.

00:02:58.313 --> 00:03:02.471
Clear field names, types and descriptions work as instructions.

00:03:02.771 --> 00:03:07.614
Total in cents, as an integer, says much more than the total amount.

00:03:07.914 --> 00:03:12.835
The model doesn't have to decide whether to write dollars, cents, or the number in

00:03:12.835 --> 00:03:13.196
words.

00:03:13.846 --> 00:03:15.350
And the error moves.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.265
Instead of an empty field that shows up weeks later, the failure appears right away,

00:03:20.265 --> 00:03:21.584
in the step that failed.

00:03:21.884 --> 00:03:24.556
Reviewers get the object and the reason.

00:03:24.856 --> 00:03:27.258
Not a paragraph to reread.

00:03:28.458 --> 00:03:29.860
Here's one for you.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.676
Your schema has a required penalty field, an integer.

00:03:33.976 --> 00:03:37.707
But half the contracts in the folder have no penalty clause at all.

00:03:38.007 --> 00:03:43.583
The eval shows a penalty filled in on those cases anyway, almost always zero, or a

00:03:43.583 --> 00:03:45.827
number copied from another field.

00:03:46.127 --> 00:03:47.244
What do you do?

00:03:50.294 --> 00:03:55.545
Make it optional, and validate in code whatever comes back filled in.

00:03:55.845 --> 00:04:01.287
A required field on a document that sometimes lacks the data pushes the model to

00:04:01.287 --> 00:04:01.967
invent it.

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:07.459
Telling the prompt to use zero when there's no penalty makes zero mean two things.

00:04:07.759 --> 00:04:10.619
A zero penalty, and a missing penalty.

00:04:10.919 --> 00:04:13.169
The next step can't tell them apart.

00:04:13.469 --> 00:04:17.610
And dropping the field throws away the half of the contracts where legal really

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.081
needs it.

00:04:18.381 --> 00:04:22.576
Absence is modeled with optional, not by deleting the field.

00:04:23.776 --> 00:04:27.055
Now the mechanics, in an order that keeps you safe.

00:04:27.705 --> 00:04:29.601
Step zero is measuring.

00:04:29.901 --> 00:04:34.659
Build an eval from real documents, with the expected value for each field.

00:04:34.959 --> 00:04:40.245
Run the current version, and record accuracy per field and the empty field rate.

00:04:40.545 --> 00:04:45.297
A field that already comes back empty twenty percent of the time, because the

00:04:45.297 --> 00:04:48.691
regular expression failed, is the first one to benefit.

00:04:49.341 --> 00:04:51.849
Then list what the next step really uses.

00:04:52.149 --> 00:04:57.918
If the system only stores the tax ID, the total, the dates and the renewal, those

00:04:57.918 --> 00:04:59.058
are your fields.

00:04:59.358 --> 00:05:03.207
Summaries, adjectives and main obligations go away.

00:05:03.857 --> 00:05:06.101
Write the schema with strong types.

00:05:06.401 --> 00:05:10.072
Integers for cents, real dates, closed lists.

00:05:10.372 --> 00:05:12.632
And a short description on each field.

00:05:12.932 --> 00:05:15.190
That description is the field's prompt.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.036
And decide honestly what's optional.

00:05:18.336 --> 00:05:22.753
Don't let the model invent an end date for a contract with no end.

00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:27.439
Then swap the call for the structured version, and delete the old parser.

00:05:27.739 --> 00:05:32.419
While both exist, someone will just fix the regular expression one more time.

00:05:33.069 --> 00:05:35.555
Add business validation in code.

00:05:35.855 --> 00:05:38.092
Line items that add up to the total.

00:05:38.392 --> 00:05:40.305
An end date after the start date.

00:05:40.605 --> 00:05:42.061
Tax ID check digits.

00:05:42.711 --> 00:05:47.799
Send failures to review, with the object and the reason, instead of saving partial

00:05:47.799 --> 00:05:48.109
data.

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:49.909
And measure again.

00:05:50.209 --> 00:05:53.248
Accuracy per field should hold or rise.

00:05:53.548 --> 00:05:56.824
Empty fields caused by parsing should drop to zero.

00:05:57.124 --> 00:06:01.868
Reading errors still exist, like the model reading twelve thousand four hundred as

00:06:01.868 --> 00:06:03.661
one thousand two hundred forty.

00:06:03.961 --> 00:06:07.090
But now they show up in validation, not weeks later.

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:09.689
A few risks to watch.

00:06:09.989 --> 00:06:12.112
A schema that forces invention.

00:06:12.412 --> 00:06:14.130
We just saw it with the penalty.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.393
A schema that's too big.

00:06:16.693 --> 00:06:19.786
Dozens of nested fields, and quality drops.

00:06:20.086 --> 00:06:25.169
The model spreads its attention and starts filling in by guesswork, usually in the

00:06:25.169 --> 00:06:26.470
fields in the middle.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:32.444
If the document has two parts, like the cover and the annexes, split it into two

00:06:32.444 --> 00:06:35.990
calls, each with what the next step actually uses.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.233
Trusting only the schema.

00:06:38.533 --> 00:06:40.780
Valid data can still be wrong.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:46.096
A total of one cent is a perfectly legal integer, and an absurd contract.

00:06:46.396 --> 00:06:51.733
Sum the line items in code, and send it to review when the numbers don't match.

00:06:52.383 --> 00:06:57.750
And some rules the provider's structured mode won't accept, like certain patterns.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:58.792
That's fine.

00:06:59.092 --> 00:07:03.443
Ask for a plain string, and validate the fourteen digits in code, right after the

00:07:03.443 --> 00:07:03.712
call.

00:07:04.362 --> 00:07:06.192
And the most common mistake.

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:11.482
Swapping the regular expression for structured output, and leaving the total typed

00:07:11.482 --> 00:07:12.212
as a string.

00:07:12.512 --> 00:07:17.583
The model is still free to return the amount with a currency symbol, as a plain

00:07:17.583 --> 00:07:18.866
number, or in words.

00:07:19.166 --> 00:07:22.461
You just moved the parser into a field with a nice name.

00:07:22.761 --> 00:07:24.959
A strong type is what closes the door.

00:07:26.159 --> 00:07:28.996
This refactoring isn't only for files.

00:07:29.296 --> 00:07:34.343
Picture two agents sharing a block of text, a kind of process scratchpad.

00:07:34.643 --> 00:07:37.300
One writes: customer ok, proceed.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.961
The other reads that as a credit approval.

00:07:41.611 --> 00:07:42.546
Same cure.

00:07:42.846 --> 00:07:47.642
The scratchpad becomes an object, with a field like credit status, from a closed

00:07:47.642 --> 00:07:51.059
list of values, validated before the next agent reads it.

00:07:51.359 --> 00:07:56.344
Prose in the middle becomes a contract, and the second agent stops interpreting

00:07:56.344 --> 00:07:56.660
okay.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.845
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:04.361
The parser that guesses is not a bug you fix one more time.

00:08:04.661 --> 00:08:06.419
It's a contract nobody wrote.

00:08:06.719 --> 00:08:11.979
Measure, write the contract as a schema with strong types, validate what a schema

00:08:11.979 --> 00:08:14.966
can't express, and delete the parser for good.

00:08:15.616 --> 00:08:20.204
In the next lesson, we look at the same extraction from another angle: the model

00:08:20.204 --> 00:08:23.530
doing math and rules that code would get right every time.

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.629
LLM doing deterministic work.

00:08:26.929 --> 00:08:27.759
See you there.
